module

public import Mathlib.Algebra.Group.Subgroup.Ker
public import Mathlib.Tactic.Group
public import Mathlib.Topology.Algebra.ContinuousMonoidHom
public import Mathlib.Topology.Algebra.Group.Subgroup
public import Mathlib.Topology.Separation.Hausdorff

/-!
# Composing presentations of a kernel as a closed normal closure

Let `h₂ : G₂ →ₜ* G₁` and `h₁ : G₁ →ₜ* G₀` be continuous homomorphisms of topological groups.
Suppose each of the two kernels is presented as the smallest closed normal subgroup containing
a given subset: `ker h₂` is the closure of the normal closure of `S₂ ⊆ G₂`, and `ker h₁` is the
closure of the normal closure of `S₁ ⊆ G₁`. This file shows that the kernel of the composite
`h₁ ∘ h₂` carries the analogous presentation, generated by `S₂` together with any set `T₂ ⊆ G₂`
of lifts of `S₁` through `h₂`, that is, any `T₂` with `h₂ '' T₂ = S₁`:

`ker (h₁ ∘ h₂) = closure ⟪⟪ S₂ ∪ T₂ ⟫⟫`.

One admissible choice is the full preimage `T₂ = h₂ ⁻¹' S₁`, but a single lift of each point of
`S₁` already suffices.

Both inclusions are elementary except for one point. For `⊇` one only needs that `ker h₁` is
closed, which the hypothesis on `h₁` supplies for free. For `⊆` one argues that the image under
`h₂` of the right-hand side `N` is a closed normal subgroup of `G₁` containing `S₁`, hence
contains `ker h₁`; so an element of `ker (h₁ ∘ h₂)` differs from an element of `N` by an element
of `ker h₂ ≤ N`. The step "the image of the closed subgroup `N` is closed" is exactly where
topology enters, and it is why `h₂` is required to be a closed map — see the implementation
notes.

Iterating the two-step statement along a chain `G n → G (n-1) → ⋯ → G 0` of continuous
surjections gives `ker_chainComp`: the kernel of the total composite `G n → G 0` is the smallest
closed normal subgroup of `G n` containing the union of the lifts, into `G n`, of all the
generating sets of the individual steps. The chain, its total composite `chainComp`, and the
total generating set `chainGens` are all indexed by `ℕ`.

This is the group-theoretic reduction left out of the proof of Stacks Tag 0BSC (Lemma 58.10.8),
where the case of finitely many removed closed points is deduced from the case of one by walking
the chain `π₁(U) → ⋯ → π₁(X \ {x₁, x₂}) → π₁(X \ {x₁}) → π₁(X)`. Nothing here is about schemes:
the statements are about topological groups only.

## Main results

* `ker_comp_eq_closedNormalClosure_of_image_eq`: the two-step composition law, for `h₂` a
  continuous surjective closed map and an arbitrary set of lifts of the generators of `ker h₁`.
* `ker_comp_eq_closedNormalClosure`: the same with the full preimage as the set of lifts.
* `ker_comp_eq_closedNormalClosure_of_compactSpace`: the same, with the closed-map hypothesis
  deduced from compactness of `G₂` and Hausdorffness of `G₁`.
* `ker_comp_eq_closedNormalClosure_union_preimage_range`: the two-step law in the shape in which
  the hypotheses are produced by `Stacks.FundamentalGroup.IsExactSequence`, i.e. with the
  generating sets taken to be the ranges of the incoming homomorphisms.
* `chainComp`, `chainGens`: the total composite `G n →ₜ* G 0` of a chain, and the total
  generating set in `G n` obtained by lifting every step's generating set.
* `ker_chainComp_succ`, `ker_chainComp`: the iterated composition law along an `n`-step chain.
* `ker_chainComp_of_compactSpace`: the iterated law for a chain of compact Hausdorff groups,
  which is the profinite case the fundamental group lives in.

## Implementation notes

The exact topological hypotheses, and why each is needed:

* `h₂` is **surjective**. Needed to know that the image of a normal subgroup is normal
  (`Subgroup.Normal.map`). It is not what lifts `S₁`: that is the hypothesis `h₂ '' T₂ = S₁`,
  which surjectivity only supplies in the special case `T₂ = h₂ ⁻¹' S₁`.
* `h₂` is a **closed map**. The hard inclusion needs `ker h₁ ≤ N.map h₂` where `N` is the
  candidate closed normal closure, and `ker h₁` is a *topological* closure; minimality of a
  topological closure (`Subgroup.topologicalClosure_minimal`) only applies against a closed
  subgroup, so `N.map h₂ = h₂ '' N` must be closed. Taking topological closures does not commute
  with `Subgroup.map` or with `Subgroup.normalClosure` for free, and this hypothesis is what
  repairs that. It is automatic when `G₂` is compact and `G₁` is Hausdorff
  (`Continuous.isClosedMap`), which is the case for profinite groups such as the étale
  fundamental group; `ker_comp_eq_closedNormalClosure_of_compactSpace` and
  `ker_chainComp_of_compactSpace` package that. Full profiniteness (total disconnectedness) is
  never used, so it is not assumed.
* `ker h₁` is **closed**. Not a separate hypothesis: it follows from the presentation of
  `ker h₁` as a topological closure.
* `h₁` is assumed only continuous. It need not be surjective, need not be closed, and `G₀` need
  not even be a topological group — only a group with a topology, as `→ₜ*` already requires.

For the iterated form the chain is indexed by all of `ℕ`, with `f i : G (i + 1) →ₜ* G i`. A
genuinely finite chain `G n → ⋯ → G 0` is extended to an `ℕ`-indexed one by taking the identity
for the steps above `n`, which is surjective, closed, and has kernel the closed normal closure of
`∅`; so nothing is lost. The total generating set is defined by the recursion
`chainGens f S (n + 1) = S n ∪ f n ⁻¹' chainGens f S n`, which unwinds, via
`Set.preimage_union`, to the union of `S n` with the iterated-preimage lift of `S i` into
`G (n + 1)` for every `i < n`; this is the sense in which it is "the union of the lifts of all the
steps' generating sets".

`ker_chainComp_succ` covers a chain of `n + 1` steps and needs no separation hypothesis at all.
`ker_chainComp` states the same for every `n : ℕ` including `n = 0`, and for that degenerate case
alone it carries `[T1Space (G 0)]`: with no steps the composite is the identity, whose kernel is
`⊥`, whereas the closed normal closure of `∅` is `closure {1}`; the two agree precisely when `G 0`
is `T1`. This is free in every intended application.

The statements are phrased directly in terms of `Subgroup.normalClosure` and
`Subgroup.topologicalClosure` rather than in terms of
`Stacks.FundamentalGroup.IsExactSequence`, whose third conjunct they unfold. That definition
lives in the étale fundamental group files, which this module deliberately does not import so as
to stay pure topological group theory;
`ker_comp_eq_closedNormalClosure_union_preimage_range` is stated so that its hypotheses are
literally the third components of two `IsExactSequence` witnesses.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0BSC](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BSC) — Lemma 58.10.8, whose
  proof defers this reduction with "a group theory argument, details left out".
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0BS9](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BS9) — the exact sequence
  formalism in which the kernel of a map of fundamental groups is described as a smallest closed
  normal subgroup.
-/

@[expose] public section

namespace Stacks.FundamentalGroup

universe u

section TwoStep

variable {G₂ G₁ G₀ : Type*}
  [Group G₂] [TopologicalSpace G₂] [IsTopologicalGroup G₂]
  [Group G₁] [TopologicalSpace G₁] [IsTopologicalGroup G₁]
  [Group G₀] [TopologicalSpace G₀]

/-- A set lies in the closed normal subgroup it generates. -/
private theorem subset_topologicalClosure_normalClosure (S : Set G₂) :
    S ⊆ (Subgroup.normalClosure S).topologicalClosure := fun _ hx =>
  Subgroup.le_topologicalClosure _ (Subgroup.subset_normalClosure hx)

/-- **Composition law for kernels presented as closed normal closures.**

Let `h₂ : G₂ →ₜ* G₁` be a surjective closed map and `h₁ : G₁ →ₜ* G₀` continuous. Suppose `ker h₂`
is the smallest closed normal subgroup of `G₂` containing `S₂`, and `ker h₁` is the smallest
closed normal subgroup of `G₁` containing `S₁`. Let `T₂ ⊆ G₂` be *any* set of lifts of `S₁`
through `h₂`, meaning `h₂ '' T₂ = S₁`. Then `ker (h₁ ∘ h₂)` is the smallest closed normal
subgroup of `G₂` containing `S₂ ∪ T₂`.

Only the single set of lifts is needed, not the whole preimage; see
`ker_comp_eq_closedNormalClosure` for that weaker special case. -/
theorem ker_comp_eq_closedNormalClosure_of_image_eq (h₂ : G₂ →ₜ* G₁) (h₁ : G₁ →ₜ* G₀)
    (hsurj : Function.Surjective h₂) (hclosed : IsClosedMap h₂)
    {S₂ T₂ : Set G₂} {S₁ : Set G₁} (himg : h₂ '' T₂ = S₁)
    (hker₂ : MonoidHom.ker h₂.toMonoidHom = (Subgroup.normalClosure S₂).topologicalClosure)
    (hker₁ : MonoidHom.ker h₁.toMonoidHom = (Subgroup.normalClosure S₁).topologicalClosure) :
    MonoidHom.ker (h₁.toMonoidHom.comp h₂.toMonoidHom) =
      (Subgroup.normalClosure (S₂ ∪ T₂)).topologicalClosure := by
  set N : Subgroup G₂ := (Subgroup.normalClosure (S₂ ∪ T₂)).topologicalClosure
  have hNnormal : N.Normal := Subgroup.is_normal_topologicalClosure _
  have hNclosed : IsClosed (N : Set G₂) := Subgroup.isClosed_topologicalClosure _
  have hgen : S₂ ∪ T₂ ⊆ N := subset_topologicalClosure_normalClosure _
  have hS₁ker : S₁ ⊆ MonoidHom.ker h₁.toMonoidHom := by
    rw [hker₁]
    exact subset_topologicalClosure_normalClosure _
  have hker₁closed : IsClosed (MonoidHom.ker h₁.toMonoidHom : Set G₁) := by
    rw [hker₁]
    exact Subgroup.isClosed_topologicalClosure _
  rw [← MonoidHom.comap_ker]
  refine le_antisymm ?_ ?_
  · -- `ker h₂ ≤ N`, because `S₂` is part of the generating set.
    have hker₂le : MonoidHom.ker h₂.toMonoidHom ≤ N := by
      rw [hker₂]
      exact Subgroup.topologicalClosure_mono
        (Subgroup.normalClosure_mono Set.subset_union_left)
    -- `h₂ '' N` is a closed normal subgroup of `G₁` containing `S₁`, hence contains `ker h₁`.
    have hMnormal : (N.map h₂.toMonoidHom).Normal := hNnormal.map _ hsurj
    have hMclosed : IsClosed ((N.map h₂.toMonoidHom : Subgroup G₁) : Set G₁) := by
      rw [Subgroup.coe_map]
      exact hclosed _ hNclosed
    have hS₁ : S₁ ⊆ (N.map h₂.toMonoidHom : Subgroup G₁) := by
      rw [← himg]
      rintro _ ⟨y, hyT, rfl⟩
      exact Subgroup.mem_map.2 ⟨y, hgen (Set.mem_union_right _ hyT), rfl⟩
    have hker₁le : MonoidHom.ker h₁.toMonoidHom ≤ N.map h₂.toMonoidHom := by
      rw [hker₁]
      exact Subgroup.topologicalClosure_minimal _
        (Subgroup.normalClosure_le_normal hS₁) hMclosed
    -- An element of the composite kernel differs from an element of `N` by an element of `ker h₂`.
    intro x hx
    obtain ⟨y, hyN, hy⟩ := Subgroup.mem_map.1 (hker₁le (hx : h₂ x ∈ _))
    have hxy : x * y⁻¹ ∈ MonoidHom.ker h₂.toMonoidHom := by
      rw [MonoidHom.mem_ker]
      change h₂ (x * y⁻¹) = 1
      rw [map_mul, map_inv, show h₂ y = h₂ x from hy]
      exact mul_inv_cancel _
    rw [show x = x * y⁻¹ * y by group]
    exact N.mul_mem (hker₂le hxy) hyN
  · -- The composite kernel is a closed normal subgroup containing the generating set.
    have hcnormal : ((MonoidHom.ker h₁.toMonoidHom).comap h₂.toMonoidHom).Normal :=
      Subgroup.Normal.comap inferInstance _
    have hcclosed : IsClosed
        (((MonoidHom.ker h₁.toMonoidHom).comap h₂.toMonoidHom : Subgroup G₂) : Set G₂) := by
      rw [Subgroup.coe_comap]
      exact hker₁closed.preimage (map_continuous h₂)
    refine Subgroup.topologicalClosure_minimal _
      (Subgroup.normalClosure_le_normal ?_) hcclosed
    rintro x (hx | hx)
    · have hmem : x ∈ MonoidHom.ker h₂.toMonoidHom := by
        rw [hker₂]
        exact subset_topologicalClosure_normalClosure _ hx
      have hx1 : h₂ x = 1 := hmem
      change h₂ x ∈ MonoidHom.ker h₁.toMonoidHom
      rw [hx1]
      exact one_mem _
    · exact hS₁ker (himg ▸ Set.mem_image_of_mem _ hx)

/-- The composition law of `ker_comp_eq_closedNormalClosure_of_image_eq` with the canonical
choice of lifts, the full preimage: if `ker h₂` is the smallest closed normal subgroup of `G₂`
containing `S₂` and `ker h₁` is the smallest closed normal subgroup of `G₁` containing `S₁`, then
`ker (h₁ ∘ h₂)` is the smallest closed normal subgroup of `G₂` containing `S₂` together with
every `h₂`-preimage of a point of `S₁`. -/
theorem ker_comp_eq_closedNormalClosure (h₂ : G₂ →ₜ* G₁) (h₁ : G₁ →ₜ* G₀)
    (hsurj : Function.Surjective h₂) (hclosed : IsClosedMap h₂)
    {S₂ : Set G₂} {S₁ : Set G₁}
    (hker₂ : MonoidHom.ker h₂.toMonoidHom = (Subgroup.normalClosure S₂).topologicalClosure)
    (hker₁ : MonoidHom.ker h₁.toMonoidHom = (Subgroup.normalClosure S₁).topologicalClosure) :
    MonoidHom.ker (h₁.toMonoidHom.comp h₂.toMonoidHom) =
      (Subgroup.normalClosure (S₂ ∪ h₂ ⁻¹' S₁)).topologicalClosure :=
  ker_comp_eq_closedNormalClosure_of_image_eq h₂ h₁ hsurj hclosed
    (Set.image_preimage_eq S₁ hsurj) hker₂ hker₁

/-- The composition law `ker_comp_eq_closedNormalClosure` for a continuous surjection from a
compact group onto a Hausdorff group: such a map is automatically closed, so no closedness
hypothesis is needed. This is the form that applies to profinite groups. -/
theorem ker_comp_eq_closedNormalClosure_of_compactSpace [CompactSpace G₂] [T2Space G₁]
    (h₂ : G₂ →ₜ* G₁) (h₁ : G₁ →ₜ* G₀) (hsurj : Function.Surjective h₂)
    {S₂ : Set G₂} {S₁ : Set G₁}
    (hker₂ : MonoidHom.ker h₂.toMonoidHom = (Subgroup.normalClosure S₂).topologicalClosure)
    (hker₁ : MonoidHom.ker h₁.toMonoidHom = (Subgroup.normalClosure S₁).topologicalClosure) :
    MonoidHom.ker (h₁.toMonoidHom.comp h₂.toMonoidHom) =
      (Subgroup.normalClosure (S₂ ∪ h₂ ⁻¹' S₁)).topologicalClosure :=
  ker_comp_eq_closedNormalClosure h₂ h₁ hsurj (map_continuous h₂).isClosedMap hker₂ hker₁

/-- The composition law in the shape supplied by two stacked exact sequences
`G₃ → G₂ → G₁` and `G₁' → G₁ → G₀`: the generating sets are the ranges of the incoming
homomorphisms, which is exactly the third component of
`Stacks.FundamentalGroup.IsExactSequence`. The kernel of `G₂ → G₀` is then the smallest closed
normal subgroup of `G₂` containing the range of `h₃` together with the full `h₂`-preimage of the
range of `h₁'`. -/
theorem ker_comp_eq_closedNormalClosure_union_preimage_range {G₃ G₁' : Type*}
    [Group G₃] [TopologicalSpace G₃] [Group G₁'] [TopologicalSpace G₁']
    (h₃ : G₃ →ₜ* G₂) (h₂ : G₂ →ₜ* G₁) (h₁' : G₁' →ₜ* G₁) (h₁ : G₁ →ₜ* G₀)
    (hsurj : Function.Surjective h₂) (hclosed : IsClosedMap h₂)
    (hker₂ : MonoidHom.ker h₂.toMonoidHom =
      (Subgroup.normalClosure (Set.range h₃)).topologicalClosure)
    (hker₁ : MonoidHom.ker h₁.toMonoidHom =
      (Subgroup.normalClosure (Set.range h₁')).topologicalClosure) :
    MonoidHom.ker (h₁.toMonoidHom.comp h₂.toMonoidHom) =
      (Subgroup.normalClosure (Set.range h₃ ∪ h₂ ⁻¹' Set.range h₁')).topologicalClosure :=
  ker_comp_eq_closedNormalClosure h₂ h₁ hsurj hclosed hker₂ hker₁

end TwoStep

section Chain

variable {G : ℕ → Type u} [∀ i, Group (G i)] [∀ i, TopologicalSpace (G i)]

/-- The total composite `G n →ₜ* G 0` of the chain of continuous homomorphisms
`f i : G (i + 1) →ₜ* G i`, that is, `f 0 ∘ f 1 ∘ ⋯ ∘ f (n - 1)`. For `n = 0` it is the
identity of `G 0`. -/
def chainComp (f : ∀ i, G (i + 1) →ₜ* G i) : (n : ℕ) → (G n →ₜ* G 0)
  | 0 => ContinuousMonoidHom.id (G 0)
  | n + 1 => (chainComp f n).comp (f n)

/-- The total generating set in `G n` of a chain whose `i`-th step has generating set
`S i ⊆ G (i + 1)`: the union of `S (n - 1)` with the lift along `f (n - 1)` of the total
generating set one level down. Unwinding the recursion, this is the union of the iterated
`f`-preimages in `G n` of all of `S 0, …, S (n - 1)`. -/
def chainGens (f : ∀ i, G (i + 1) →ₜ* G i) (S : ∀ i, Set (G (i + 1))) : (n : ℕ) → Set (G n)
  | 0 => ∅
  | n + 1 => S n ∪ f n ⁻¹' chainGens f S n

/-- The empty composite of a chain is the identity of `G 0`. -/
@[simp]
theorem chainComp_zero (f : ∀ i, G (i + 1) →ₜ* G i) :
    chainComp f 0 = ContinuousMonoidHom.id (G 0) := rfl

/-- The composite of a chain of `n + 1` steps is the composite of its first `n` steps with the
last one. -/
@[simp]
theorem chainComp_succ (f : ∀ i, G (i + 1) →ₜ* G i) (n : ℕ) :
    chainComp f (n + 1) = (chainComp f n).comp (f n) := rfl

/-- An empty chain has empty total generating set. -/
@[simp]
theorem chainGens_zero (f : ∀ i, G (i + 1) →ₜ* G i) (S : ∀ i, Set (G (i + 1))) :
    chainGens f S 0 = (∅ : Set (G 0)) := rfl

/-- The total generating set of a chain of `n + 1` steps is the last step's generating set
together with the lift of the total generating set of the first `n` steps. -/
@[simp]
theorem chainGens_succ (f : ∀ i, G (i + 1) →ₜ* G i) (S : ∀ i, Set (G (i + 1))) (n : ℕ) :
    chainGens f S (n + 1) = S n ∪ f n ⁻¹' chainGens f S n := rfl

variable [∀ i, IsTopologicalGroup (G i)]

/-- **Iterated composition law, for a chain of `n + 1` steps.**

Along a chain of continuous surjective closed maps `f i : G (i + 1) →ₜ* G i`, each of whose
kernels is the smallest closed normal subgroup containing `S i`, the kernel of the total
composite `G (n + 1) →ₜ* G 0` is the smallest closed normal subgroup of `G (n + 1)` containing
the union `chainGens f S (n + 1)` of the lifts of all the `S i` with `i ≤ n`. -/
theorem ker_chainComp_succ (f : ∀ i, G (i + 1) →ₜ* G i) (S : ∀ i, Set (G (i + 1)))
    (hsurj : ∀ i, Function.Surjective (f i)) (hclosed : ∀ i, IsClosedMap (f i))
    (hker : ∀ i, MonoidHom.ker (f i).toMonoidHom =
      (Subgroup.normalClosure (S i)).topologicalClosure) (n : ℕ) :
    MonoidHom.ker (chainComp f (n + 1)).toMonoidHom =
      (Subgroup.normalClosure (chainGens f S (n + 1))).topologicalClosure := by
  induction n with
  | zero =>
    have hg : chainGens f S 1 = S 0 := by simp
    have hc : (chainComp f 1).toMonoidHom = (f 0).toMonoidHom := MonoidHom.id_comp _
    rw [hg, hc, hker 0]
  | succ n ih =>
    exact ker_comp_eq_closedNormalClosure (f (n + 1)) (chainComp f (n + 1))
      (hsurj (n + 1)) (hclosed (n + 1)) (hker (n + 1)) ih

/-- **Iterated composition law, for a chain of `n` steps for every `n : ℕ`.**

As `ker_chainComp_succ`, but covering also the empty chain `n = 0`, where the composite is the
identity of `G 0` and both sides are the trivial subgroup. The hypothesis `[T1Space (G 0)]` is
used for that degenerate case only: it is what makes the closed normal closure of `∅` trivial. -/
theorem ker_chainComp [T1Space (G 0)] (f : ∀ i, G (i + 1) →ₜ* G i) (S : ∀ i, Set (G (i + 1)))
    (hsurj : ∀ i, Function.Surjective (f i)) (hclosed : ∀ i, IsClosedMap (f i))
    (hker : ∀ i, MonoidHom.ker (f i).toMonoidHom =
      (Subgroup.normalClosure (S i)).topologicalClosure) (n : ℕ) :
    MonoidHom.ker (chainComp f n).toMonoidHom =
      (Subgroup.normalClosure (chainGens f S n)).topologicalClosure := by
  cases n with
  | zero =>
    have hbot : (Subgroup.normalClosure (∅ : Set (G 0))).topologicalClosure = ⊥ := by
      rw [Subgroup.normalClosure_empty]
      refine SetLike.coe_injective ?_
      rw [Subgroup.coe_topologicalClosure_bot, Subgroup.coe_bot, closure_singleton]
    rw [chainGens_zero, hbot, chainComp_zero]
    exact MonoidHom.ker_id
  | succ n => exact ker_chainComp_succ f S hsurj hclosed hker n

/-- The iterated composition law `ker_chainComp` for a chain of compact Hausdorff groups: every
continuous surjection between them is automatically closed, so only surjectivity is required.
This is the form that applies to a chain of profinite groups. -/
theorem ker_chainComp_of_compactSpace [∀ i, CompactSpace (G i)] [∀ i, T2Space (G i)]
    (f : ∀ i, G (i + 1) →ₜ* G i) (S : ∀ i, Set (G (i + 1)))
    (hsurj : ∀ i, Function.Surjective (f i))
    (hker : ∀ i, MonoidHom.ker (f i).toMonoidHom =
      (Subgroup.normalClosure (S i)).topologicalClosure) (n : ℕ) :
    MonoidHom.ker (chainComp f n).toMonoidHom =
      (Subgroup.normalClosure (chainGens f S n)).topologicalClosure :=
  ker_chainComp f S hsurj (fun i => (map_continuous (f i)).isClosedMap) hker n

end Chain

end Stacks.FundamentalGroup

end
